Third-party material

src/castellan/data/lookalikes.py and src/castellan/data/README.md quote short
allowlist patterns and example values from the projects below, to build benign
lookalike training documents. Each quote is cited in the docstring of the
category that uses it.

  Gitleaks
    https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks
    Default config, tag v8.30.1 (commit 83d9cd684c87d95d656c1458ef04895a7f1cbd8e),
    config/gitleaks.toml: global allowlist regexes, paths and stopwords, and
    rule-level allowlists (generic-api-key, curl-auth-user, aws-access-token,
    gcp-api-key, private-key).
    Copyright (c) 2019 Zachary Rice
    Licensed under the MIT License.

  detect-secrets
    https://github.com/Yelp/detect-secrets
    Commit 5e141933554a0b74e7341841f318be21e895339c,
    detect_secrets/filters/heuristic.py: heuristic filter names and patterns
    (is_templated_secret, is_indirect_reference, is_potential_uuid,
    is_likely_id_string, is_sequential_string, is_not_alphanumeric_string,
    is_lock_file, is_prefixed_with_dollar_sign).
    By Yelp, Inc. and contributors.
    Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

Documented example values, used as published, from public documentation:
  - AWS IAM and AWS CLI user guides (example access key id and secret key)
  - RFC 7519, section 3.1 (example JWT)
  - RFC 2397 (data URI examples)
  - Slack API docs, "Cursor-based pagination" (example cursors)

Gitleaks (CLI, MIT) and Presidio (presidio-analyzer, MIT) with the spaCy model
en_core_web_lg (MIT) are used as baselines. They are installed separately and
not included in this repository. Qwen3 model weights (Apache-2.0) are downloaded
from Hugging Face at run time and not included.
